WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.046 --> 00:00:10.526
Skyler surveyed the clean beach and let out a sigh of relief.

2
00:00:10.527 --> 00:00:15.122
Even somber Walter showed a hint of a smile.

3
00:00:15.123 --> 00:00:21.060
Skyler had helped to organize a beach cleanup for her school's Green Club.

4
00:00:21.061 --> 00:00:30.432
She had only recently joined the club, after gaining an interest in environmental issues, and eagerly volunteered to help plan the effort.

5
00:00:30.433 --> 00:00:35.876
The Green Club had teamed up with a local private school for the first time.

6
00:00:35.877 --> 00:00:43.877
Skyler was definitely impressed by Walter, the president of the private school's environmental club.

7
00:00:43.878 --> 00:00:54.032
Walter had not only gotten plastic utensils and straws banned from his school's cafeteria, but he'd also gotten the two schools together.

8
00:00:54.033 --> 00:01:03.030
But there was one thing about working with Walter that was challenging—he was always so serious!

9
00:01:03.031 --> 00:01:10.677
"This is going to look great for our yearbook," Skyler said excitedly, snapping a picture of the beach.

10
00:01:10.678 --> 00:01:15.865
"It was a long morning, but we cleaned up the entire beach!

11
00:01:15.866 --> 00:01:21.138
Now it looks almost pristine."

12
00:01:21.139 --> 00:01:27.579
"We did a lot of work, but there's still plastic all around," Walter said.

13
00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:31.725
"Huh?" Skyler furrowed her brow.

14
00:01:31.726 --> 00:01:38.982
The students had collected a huge amount of plastic debris—wrappers, bottles, and bags.

15
00:01:38.983 --> 00:01:46.491
Club members were now carefully combing through it, checking to see what was recyclable and what was not.

16
00:01:46.492 --> 00:01:52.665
"I don't see anymore plastic," she mumbled.

17
00:01:52.666 --> 00:01:58.651
"You might not see it, but that doesn't mean it's not there," Walter said.

18
00:01:58.652 --> 00:02:02.520
"Haven't you heard of microplastics?"

19
00:02:02.521 --> 00:02:11.259
"Oh!" Skyler said. "I've heard of microplastics, but I don't really know much about them."

20
00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:16.808
"Microplastics are tiny pieces of plastic less than five millimeters in size.

21
00:02:16.809 --> 00:02:22.627
Looking at the beach now, it's not obvious that it's still polluted with plastic.

22
00:02:22.628 --> 00:02:27.542
That's because microplastics are so tiny."

23
00:02:27.543 --> 00:02:32.676
"Where do microplastics come from?" Skyler asked.

24
00:02:32.677 --> 00:02:37.750
"They come from nearly anything that has plastic," Walter said.

25
00:02:37.751 --> 00:02:46.406
"Even though plastic may take hundreds of years to break down—which is why we need to lessen our consumption—it still breaks down.

26
00:02:46.407 --> 00:02:51.299
And when it does, tiny fragments are released into the water, soil, and air."

27
00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:54.135
"Air?"

28
00:02:54.136 --> 00:03:00.571
"Yeah. Microplastics are even in the air, which means we breathe them in.

29
00:03:00.572 --> 00:03:05.820
Since the particles are so small, they can easily travel through the air like dust.

30
00:03:05.821 --> 00:03:11.799
Or they come from synthetic fibers like polyester that get shed from clothes.

31
00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:18.947
These fibers also enter the water when we do laundry." Walter sighed.

32
00:03:18.948 --> 00:03:24.378
"Did you know that microplastics are in the food chain too?"

33
00:03:24.379 --> 00:03:26.964
Skyler shook her head.

34
00:03:26.965 --> 00:03:32.705
"A lot of microplastics get consumed by seafood," Walter continued.

35
00:03:32.706 --> 00:03:38.662
"Small sea animals ingest microplastics, then larger fish eat them.

36
00:03:38.663 --> 00:03:41.533
And then we eat those fish!

37
00:03:41.534 --> 00:03:48.322
What's worse is that the chemicals used to make plastic can be toxic and even cause cancer.

38
00:03:48.323 --> 00:03:53.292
All this stuff ends up in our bodies."

39
00:03:53.293 --> 00:04:01.668
"I thought I knew a lot about saving the planet," Skyler said, "but I guess there's still a lot to learn."

40
00:04:01.669 --> 00:04:09.052
Walter nodded. "Microplastics aren't new, but our understanding of them is.

41
00:04:09.053 --> 00:04:13.781
And many people still don't see how prevalent the problem really is."

42
00:04:13.782 --> 00:04:19.143
"I wonder what can be done," Skyler said.

43
00:04:19.144 --> 00:04:24.527
"Well, cleaning up the beach was a good thing to do," Walter said.

44
00:04:24.528 --> 00:04:28.836
"It will prevent some microplastics from contaminating the beach.

45
00:04:28.837 --> 00:04:33.611
We should all recycle, of course, and try to use less plastic.

46
00:04:33.612 --> 00:04:37.297
Or, we can use biodegradable plastic instead.

47
00:04:37.298 --> 00:04:41.974
New plastics are being made out of biodegradable materials."

48
00:04:41.975 --> 00:04:51.722
"But they might not be as biodegradable as we think, and some use more materials to make than regular plastics," Skyler said.

49
00:04:51.723 --> 00:05:00.464
"It's also easier for wealthy people to use less plastic or buy different products, but not everyone can afford to do that.

50
00:05:00.465 --> 00:05:03.973
Green products are usually more expensive.

51
00:05:03.974 --> 00:05:10.206
It's hard to tell people to use less plastic when the alternatives are too expensive for them."

52
00:05:10.207 --> 00:05:15.689
"Right, and some products labeled 'green' aren't really green at all.

53
00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:23.447
Companies just use green packaging or earth-friendly words to sell things at higher prices."

54
00:05:23.448 --> 00:05:26.187
"That's called greenwashing."

55
00:05:26.188 --> 00:05:34.635
Walter nodded. "Yeah. I convinced my mom to use new floor wipes because the box looked eco-friendly.

56
00:05:34.636 --> 00:05:39.084
But later I read that they weren't any different from the other wipes.

57
00:05:39.085 --> 00:05:44.068
That made me mad." He shook his head.

58
00:05:44.069 --> 00:05:47.623
"Saving the earth is the right thing for all of us to do.

59
00:05:47.624 --> 00:05:53.439
But lots of corporations treat it as a way to make more money."

60
00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:57.806
"Our planet is running out of time," Skyler said.

61
00:05:57.807 --> 00:06:02.030
"I hope we can find some solutions before it's too late."

62
00:06:02.031 --> 00:06:07.505
Walter glanced up at the other kids who were still busy sorting plastic.

63
00:06:07.506 --> 00:06:10.829
"There aren't any easy solutions.

64
00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:16.024
But you and I have joined these clubs, and today's project will make a difference.

65
00:06:16.025 --> 00:06:22.156
And right now there's something else we can do." Walter grinned.

66
00:06:22.157 --> 00:06:26.590
"Help the other kids! They probably think we're slacking off!"

67
00:06:26.591 --> 00:06:31.450
Skyler smiled back, pleased to see Walter lightening up.

68
00:06:31.451 --> 00:06:36.223
As they headed toward the group of students, Walter suddenly stopped.

69
00:06:36.224 --> 00:06:41.027
"Maybe our school clubs can collaborate more often," he said.

70
00:06:41.028 --> 00:06:47.168
Skyler's smile widened. "Great idea!"

